    "created_at": "2016-03-18T16:34:01Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-03-18T16:34:01Z",
    "body": "My proposal, if implementers are willing to take the back-compat risk, is that the element interface for (\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\", \"script\") becomes `HTMLScriptElement`. Optionally, we can add an alias `window.SVGScriptElement = window.HTMLScriptElement` like we've done for `Document` and `HTMLDocument`.\n\nThis has the following potentially-negative impacts:\n- It breaks the invariant that all `HTMLElement` instances have the HTML namespace as their namespace. (I can't think of anything relying on this, but maybe I am not thinking hard enough.)\n- It breaks the invariant that anything in the SVG namespace inherits from `SVGElement`. (Again, I can't see any bad consequences of this.)\n- Several properties get removed:\n  - ownerSVGElement\n  - viewportElement\n  - href (a SVGAnimatedString)\n- The className property becomes a DOMString instead of a SVGAnimatedString.\n- Many new properties get added by inheriting from HTMLElement.\n\nI'm not sure if this is acceptable to implementers or the SVGWG. But it seems plausible to me.\n\nIf we can't get away with this then I'm not sure we should define SVGScriptElement in HTML. It has enough SVG-specific foibles and dependencies that it doesn't seem to be in our wheelhouse. Instead we should just make sure HTMLScriptElement is well-factored enough that the SVG editors can reference it for any behavior they want to copy.\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
